{"qid":"Q786","names":["Dominican Republic"],"title":"Dominican Republic","endonym":{"native":"República Dominicana","lang":"es"},"capsule":"The Dominican Republic declared independence from Haiti on 27 February 1844 under the leadership of Juan Pablo Duarte, Francisco del Rosario Sánchez, and Ramón Matías Mella, establishing the first republic in the Caribbean to begin its existence as a sovereign state rather than a former European colony. The country endured repeated instability — including a voluntary annexation to Spain 1861–1865, a subsequent War of Restoration, and US military occupation 1916–1924 — before the dictatorship of Rafael Trujillo (1930–1961) imposed three decades of brutal authoritarian rule. After Trujillo's assassination democratic transitions remained fragile until the 1978 electoral handover from Joaquín Balaguer to Antonio Guzmán marked the first peaceful transfer of power. The republic occupies the eastern two-thirds of Hispaniola.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The land territory of the Dominican Republic is precisely defined by its border with Haiti and international recognition; the border was finalized through successive bilateral agreements, with some adjustments as late as 1936.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Dominican Republic","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Dominican-Republic"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Dominican Republic","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dominican_Republic"}],"britannica":"place/Dominican-Republic","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Dominican Republic","rulers":[{"qid":"Q6291505","name":"José Antonio Salcedo","start":1863,"end":1864,